1. The Big Picture: How Security Controls Fit Together
Think of a request arriving at your API. The controls are applied in this exact order:

Rate limiting – Stops an attacker (or a buggy client) from overwhelming your server.

Encryption (HTTPS) – Protects data in transit from eavesdropping and tampering.

Authentication – Verifies who the user is (using passwords).

Audit logging – Records every request (who did what and when).

Access control – Checks if the authenticated user has permission for this action.

This ordering is deliberate:

Rate limiting must happen first because it uses minimal resources and protects everything else.

Authentication doesn’t reject a request immediately if it fails; it just leaves the request “anonymous.” That way audit logging still records the attempt.

Access control can then reject anonymous (or unauthorized) requests with a 401 or 403 status.

Key concept: Only rate limiting and access control directly reject requests. Everything else runs in the background.

2. Rate Limiting – Stopping DoS Attacks
Why? Attackers can flood your API with millions of requests, crashing it. Rate limiting ensures you only process what your server can handle.

How? Use Google Guava’s RateLimiter. It’s a simple library that lets you say “allow only X requests per second.”

Implementation steps:

Add Guava dependency to pom.xml.

Create a RateLimiter object early in your main method.

Use a Spark before filter (the very first filter!) to check if the limit is exceeded. If yes, immediately halt with 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header (so the client knows when to try again).

Example code snippet:

java
var rateLimiter = RateLimiter.create(2.0d); // 2 requests per second

before((request, response) -> {
    if (!rateLimiter.tryAcquire()) {
        response.header("Retry-After", "2");
        halt(429);
    }
});
Production tip: Put rate limiting on a load balancer/reverse proxy if possible, but keep a per‑server limit as defense in depth.

3. Authentication – Proving Who You Are
Why? Without authentication, anyone can pretend to be someone else (spoofing).

Approach: HTTP Basic Authentication + secure password storage with Scrypt.

3.1 How Basic Auth Works
The client sends an Authorization header:
Authorization: Basic ZGVtbzpjaGFuZ2VpdA==
That string is username:password encoded in Base64. (Not encrypted! That’s why HTTPS is mandatory.)

3.2 Storing Passwords Securely
Never store raw passwords. Use a password hashing function like Scrypt. It’s deliberately slow and memory‑hard, so attackers can’t quickly try millions of guesses even if they steal the database.

Each password gets a unique random salt (so two users with the same password have different hashes).

The Scrypt library stores the salt, cost parameters, and hash in one string. When checking a password, it extracts the salt and parameters, re‑hashes, and compares.

Creating a user (in UserController.registerUser):

Validate username (pattern [a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]{1,29}) and password length (≥ 8).

Hash password:
SCryptUtil.scrypt(password, 32768, 8, 1)
(Values chosen for ~100ms on a single CPU; adjust only if absolutely necessary.)

Store username and hash in the users table using a prepared statement (prevents SQL injection).

3.3 Authenticating Every Request
Add a Spark before filter that:

Reads the Authorization header (if present and starts with “Basic ”).

Decodes the Base64 to get username:password.

Looks up the hash in the database by user_id.

Uses SCryptUtil.check(password, hash) to verify.

If successful, sets request.attribute("subject", username).

Important: This filter does not reject the request on failure. That’s for access control later.

Then in your business logic (e.g., creating a space or posting a message), check that the owner/author field matches the subject attribute.

4. Encryption – Protecting Data in Transit (and at Rest)
Why? Without encryption, passwords and messages travel over the network in clear text—anyone on the same WiFi can read them.

How? Enable HTTPS (HTTP over TLS). Spark makes it easy:

java
secure("localhost.p12", "changeit", null, null);
Get a certificate:

Use mkcert to create a locally trusted CA and generate a PKCS#12 certificate for localhost.

Never use self‑signed certs in production; for development, mkcert works smoothly because your browser trusts its CA.

Bonus: Add an HTTP Strict‑Transport‑Security (HSTS) header to tell browsers to always use HTTPS:

java
response.header("Strict-Transport-Security", "max-age=31536000");
(Not needed for pure APIs, but good if you ever serve web pages.)

Encrypt at rest is also mentioned (database encryption) but covered in chapter 5.

5. Audit Logging – Who Did What and When
Why? Accountability. If something bad happens, you need to trace it. Also logs failed attempts (potential attacks).

Where? After authentication, before access control. So you log even attempts that get denied.

Implementation:

Create an audit_log table (with columns: audit_id, method, path, user_id, status, audit_time).

Two filters:

auditRequestStart – runs before request processing; generates a unique audit_id and inserts a row (status NULL).

auditRequestEnd – runs after; updates the same audit_id with the response status.

This pair lets you correlate a request with its response and see if the server crashed mid‑processing (no end log).

Reading logs: Provide a secured (or at least restricted) endpoint to view recent logs. In the book, they use a simple SELECT with a 1‑hour window and limit.

Principle of separation of duties: The people who review audit logs (auditors) should be different from the system administrators who could otherwise cover their tracks.

6. Access Control – Enforcing Permissions
Why? You’ve proven who the user is, but should they be allowed to read, post, or delete in this space? Access control (authorization) enforces that.

Key distinction:

401 Unauthorized – “I don’t know who you are.” (Authentication failed.)

403 Forbidden – “I know who you are, but you’re not allowed to do that.” (Authorization failed.)

6.1 Require Authentication for All /spaces Routes
A filter that checks request.attribute("subject") isn’t null. If null → halt(401) with a WWW-Authenticate header.

6.2 Simple Access Control Lists (ACL)
Create a permissions table linking space_id, user_id, and a string like "r", "w", "d" (read, write, delete). When a space is created, grant "rwd" to the owner.

To enforce permissions, a filter factory method requirePermission(method, permission) returns a Spark Filter. Inside that filter:

If the HTTP method doesn’t match, do nothing (let it pass to another filter).

Else, call requireAuthentication, then look up the user’s permissions for :spaceId.

If the permission string does not contain the required letter → halt(403).

Then you attach that filter to specific routes in Main:

java
before("/spaces/:spaceId/messages", userController.requirePermission("GET", "r"));
get("/spaces/:spaceId/messages", spaceController::findMessages);
6.3 Adding Members – Watch Out for Privilege Escalation
When adding a member, the addMember method originally required only read ("r") permission. That meant a user with read‑only access could invite a new user with full "rwd" permissions—effectively giving themselves admin rights. Fix: Change the required permission to "rwd" so only moderators can add members. Always check that the permissions you grant don’t exceed what the granter themselves have.

Summary: The Full Request Flow
Here’s a visual recap of what happens when a request hits your API:

text
Request arrives
  → Rate limiter (reject if over limit, 429)
  → HTTPS decrypts and verifies integrity
  → Authentication filter (sets subject if credentials valid, else null)
  → Audit start log
  → Access control filter (check subject & permissions; halt 401/403 if needed)
  → Business logic
  → Audit end log
  → Response encrypted by HTTPS
By implementing these five controls, you’ve protected against the six STRIDE threats:

Spoofing → Authentication

Tampering → Encryption (HTTPS) + Access control

Repudiation → Audit logging

Information disclosure → Encryption + Access control

Denial of service → Rate limiting

Elevation of privilege → Access control (properly designed)

